    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-20878",
      "problem": "Sketch the graph of the function $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/a0bbb2611a949209a964f833eefe4870dc7df948bd88dd01567ddc493046be30.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the right to give $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/31abb3ce0f7149bde6aabd69742bf01483d0c4a173efe6dc9f380b03017b1dc3.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x-4}.$ The point $(4,1)$ moves to $(4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/9815e40e53ca5ae09915c4c8ce88c75a1ca22bd52fe23e40a3b9bdf0c1051744.png)",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/observations/step-e20878-95b830385a1d4d99126e.html",
